# Integration recovery

Read the section that matches the refusal or result `spec-integrate` reports.

## Base merge after a Git refusal

Run this in the task worktree, only after the CLI reports `WORKTREE_MERGE_CONFLICT`, or `WORKTREE_MERGE_NOT_FF` under `merge = "ff-only"` or on the PR route.

1. Merge, never rebase (the unit's records cite its SHAs): `git merge -m "<subject>" <base-branch>`, from the local base{% if execution.environment == "local" %} without fetching{% endif %}.
2. When the merge stops on conflicts, run `devkit generated resolve` first: it settles conflicts in registered generated files (the installation receipt `.devkit/installation.json`, and those `devkit generated list` shows) by regenerating them; resolve only the conflicts it reports as remaining by hand.
3. Run `devkit rules fix` and commit any reference repairs after resolving a base merge.
4. Run the repository's affected checks on `git diff --name-only <pre-merge-head> HEAD` (e.g. `just test-affected --base <base-ref>`); without one, reuse the source's verification.
5. If the merge changed bundle sources, run `devkit sync` and commit its tracked changes before approval.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}
## Unknown or partial result

Inspect `devkit worktree status` and Git or PR state. If the local merge landed, run `devkit worktree finish <branch>` for only the remaining guarded cleanup.
{% endif %}
## Remote deletion failure

A remote deletion failure preserves the local worktree and branch. Inspect the merged PR and remote ref, prepare the remaining scope and retry with its new digest.
